{
  "generated_text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sqrt{3x+6} = x+2\\), we need to follow a systematic approach. Let's start by squaring both sides of the equation to eliminate the square root.\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3x+6} = x+2\n\\]\n\nSquare both sides:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{3x+6})^2 = (x+2)^2\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n3x + 6 = (x+2)^2\n\\]\n\nExpand the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n3x + 6 = x^2 + 4x + 4\n\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 4x + 4 - 3x - 6 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + x - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using factoring. We look for two numbers that multiply to \\(-2\\) and add to \\(1\\). These numbers are \\(2\\) and \\(-1\\). Thus, we can factor the quadratic equation as follows:\n\n\\[\n(x + 2)(x - 1) = 0\n\\]\n\nSet each factor equal to zero:\n\n\\[\nx + 2 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = -2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to verify these solutions in the original equation \\(\\sqrt{3x+6} = x+2\\).\n\n1. For \\(x = -2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3(-2) + 6} = -2 + 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{-6 + 6} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{0} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n0 = 0 \\quad \\text{(True)}\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(x = 1\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3(1) + 6} = 1 + 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3 + 6} = 3\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{9} = 3\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3 = 3 \\quad \\text{(True)}\n\\]\n\nBoth solutions satisfy the original equation. Therefore, the solutions are \\(x = -2\\) and \\(x = 1\\).\n\nOrdering the solutions from least to greatest, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2, 1}\n\\]",
